{"records":[{"id":"gfcn2wmrpf","title":"Dataset for the paper entitled \"A genetic map of the response to DNA damage in human cells\" by Olivieri M et al. Cell 2020","description":"We used pooled CRISPR/Cas9 screens in the human RPE1-hTERT p53-/- cell line against 27 genotoxic agents. The Dataset herein make the primary data available. The files are:\n\n* Additional genes of interest (discussion and data relating to TMEM2, ESD, USP37, PHF12, BTAF1 and DRAP1)\n* Supplementary Raw Data: Data used to make all the graphs in the manuscript\n* Folder \"CRISPR screen readcount files\". Readcounts for the screens undertaken as part of this study.\n* Folder \"Raw images for immunoblots\": uncropped images for all the blots in the manuscript\n* Folder: \"Additional QC analyses of CRISPR screens\": Additional analyses that measure the quality of the screens using Presion-Recall curves of essential genes and an estimate of genes that scored as hits due to batch effects.\n* Folder \"Data_Analysis_Code\" R markdown file along with data files\n* File: \"Additional Genes of Interests\" contains a short discussion of genes we found noteworthy after analysis of our screen data.\n\nVersion 2 note: this version differs from Version 1 by a single file (FDRPos_31screens.csv) which has a tab error fixed.\n\n\n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-07-07T14:26:40.176Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gfcn2wmrpf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gfcn2wmrpf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michele","last_name":"Olivieri","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Quality","Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ys4gwt7m6n","title":"Computational and quantitative data for Zmlaccase2 is essential for cuticle pigmentation and development in the edible insect Zophobas morio","description":"This dataset contains the processed quantitative data supporting the figures in the manuscript “Zmlaccase2 is essential for cuticle pigmentation and development in the edible insect Zophobas morio”. The Excel file includes RT-qPCR relative expression data, RNAi survival data, body weight changes, pupation rates, adult emergence rates, and body length and body weight measurements of CRISPR/Cas9-induced Zmlaccase2-edited G0 individuals. The data correspond to the qPCR analyses, RNAi functional assays, and CRISPR/Cas9 phenotypic analyses presented in the manuscript. dsGFP represents the control RNAi treatment, dsZmlaccase2 represents the Zmlaccase2 RNAi treatment, WT represents wild-type larvae, and Mut represents Zmlaccase2-edited G0 individuals.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-07-01T18:23:04.191Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ys4gwt7m6n","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ys4gwt7m6n"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"yumeng","last_name":"cheng","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiaofang","last_name":"Liang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Huiling","last_name":"Dai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yanxia","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jingyi","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Frederic","last_name":"Francis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Julian","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"jun","last_name":"xu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Min","last_name":"Xue","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Genetics","Molecular Biology","Developmental Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rhsw3nbjhz","title":"Essential gene profiles for human pluripotent stem cells identify uncharacterized genes and substrate dependencies. Mair et al.","description":"Human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) provide an invaluable tool for modeling diseases and hold promise for regenerative medicine. For understanding pluripotency and lineage differentiation mechanisms, a critical first step involves systematically cataloging genes that are indispensable for hPSC maintenance and proliferation. To map genetic determinants of hPSC fitness, we performed genome-scale loss-of-function screens in an inducible Cas9 H1 hPSC line cultured on feeder cells and feeder-free to identify essential genes. Among these, we found FOXH1 and VENTX, genes that encode transcription factors previously implicated in stem cell biology, as well as an uncharacterized gene, C22orf43/DRICH1. hPSCs essential genes are substantially different from other cell lines, and gene essentiality in hPSCs is highly context-dependent with respect to different growth substrates. Our CRISPR screens establish parameters for genome-wide screens in hPSCs, which will facilitate the characterization of unappreciated regulators of hPSC biology to understand the genetic wiring of hPSC fitness and pluripotency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-24T11:57:50.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhsw3nbjhz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhsw3nbjhz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jelena","last_name":"Tomic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Functional Genomics","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z4ht3txn2k","title":"Accompanying Files for paper entitled: \"Genome-scale mapping of DNA damage suppressors through phenotypic CRISPR/Cas9 screens\" - Zhao et al. Molecular Cell 2023","description":"Accompanying Files for paper entitled: \"Genome-scale mapping of DNA damage suppressors through phenotypic CRISPR/Cas9 screens\" - Zhao et al. Molecular Cell 2023. This dataset contains Source and additional Data for all figures.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-06-06T07:16:19.286Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z4ht3txn2k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z4ht3txn2k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Durocher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yichao","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01gavpb45","name":"Canadian Institutes of Health Research","grant_id":null}],"categories":["DNA Replication","DNA Repair","Flow Cytometry","Cancer Chemotherapy","DNA Damage","Functional Genomics","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"sxrvzkr4hh","title":"Pooled in vitro and in vivo CRISPR-Cas9 screening identifies tumor suppressors in human colon organoids","description":"UMI validation screen, original data from in vivo tumor suppressor CRISPR/Cas9 screening experiment. Figure 6.\nExperimental setup: 281 tumor suppressor gRNAs, positive controls, neutral controls and gRNAs targeting essential genes were introduced into the CRISPR-UMI lentivirus (Michlits et al., 2017). Transduced AK organoids (APC-KO and KRASG12D) were transplanted subcutaneously in NSG mice followed by barcode sequencing of the tumor pool (from 10 mice) and 5 individual tumors. Clone and read number were determined by next generation sequencing. The conventional gRNA enrichment analysis was compared to UMI-CRISPR-screening with outlier removal.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-04-20T15:46:17.868Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sxrvzkr4hh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sxrvzkr4hh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Henner","last_name":"Farin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"6bkjdg5g85","title":"Zmat3 is a key splicing regulator in the p53 tumor suppression program","description":"Although TP53 is the most commonly mutated gene in human cancers, the p53-dependent transcriptional programs mediating tumor suppression remain incompletely understood. Here, to uncover critical components downstream of p53 in tumor suppression, we perform unbiased RNAi and CRISPR/Cas9-based genetic screens in vivo.  These screens converge upon the p53-inducible gene Zmat3, encoding an RNA-binding-protein, and we demonstrate that ZMAT3 is an important tumor suppressor downstream of p53 in mouse KrasG12D-driven lung and liver cancers and human carcinomas. Integrative analysis of the ZMAT3 RNA-binding landscape and transcriptomic profiling reveals that ZMAT3 directly modulates exon inclusion in transcripts encoding proteins of diverse functions, including the p53 inhibitors MDM4 and MDM2, splicing regulators, and components of varied cellular processes. Interestingly, these exons are enriched in NMD signals, and, accordingly, ZMAT3 broadly affects target transcript stability. Collectively, these studies reveal ZMAT3 as a novel RNA-splicing and homeostasis regulator and key component of p53-mediated tumor suppression.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-06T16:30:19.918Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/6bkjdg5g85","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/6bkjdg5g85"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kathryn","last_name":"Bieging-Rolett","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Tumor","Alternative Splicing","RNA Sequencing","P53","Mouse Model of Cancer","Adenocarcinoma","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3sdsc83vsn","title":"Genome-Scale Exon Perturbation Screens Uncover Exons Critical for Cell Fitness","description":"CRISPR-Cas technology has transformed functional genomics, yet understanding how individual exons differentially shape cellular phenotypes remains limited. Here, we optimized and conducted massively parallel exon deletion and splice site mutation screens in human cell lines to identify exons that regulate cellular fitness. Fitness-promoting exons are prevalent in essential and highly expressed genes, and commonly overlap with protein domains and interaction interfaces. Conversely, fitness-suppressing exons are enriched in non-essential genes, exhibiting lower inclusion levels, and overlap with intrinsically disordered regions and disease-associated mutations. In-depth mechanistic investigation of the TAF5 alternative exon-8, screen hit, revealed that its inclusion is required for assembly of the TFIID general transcription initiation complex, thereby regulating global gene expression output. Collectively, our orthogonal exon perturbation screens established a comprehensive repository of phenotypically important exons and uncovered regulatory mechanisms governing cellular fitness and gene expression.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-20T08:55:47.576Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3sdsc83vsn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3sdsc83vsn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mei-sheng","last_name":"Xiao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Arun Prasath","last_name":"Damodaran","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bandana","last_name":"Kumari","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ethan","last_name":"Dickson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kun","last_name":"Xing","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tyler","last_name":"On","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nikhil","last_name":"Parab","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Helen E. ","last_name":"King","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexendar R.","last_name":"Perez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wilfried M. ","last_name":"Guiblet","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gerard","last_name":"Duncan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anney","last_name":"Che","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Raj","last_name":"Chari","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thorkell","last_name":"Andresson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joana A.","last_name":"Vidigal","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Robert J.","last_name":"Weatheritt","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Aregger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thomas","last_name":"Gonatopoulos-Pournatzis","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microscopy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9x47wzy8x7","title":"A study of \"Genome-wide CRISPR screens reveal host factors critical for SARS-CoV-2 infection\" by Wei et al. Cell 2020","description":"Identification of host genes essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection may reveal novel therapeutic targets and inform our understanding of COVID-19 pathogenesis. Here, we performed genome-wide CRISPR screens in Vero-E6 cells with SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV, bat coronavirus HKU5 expressing the SARS-CoV-1 spike, and VSV expressing the SARS-CoV-2 spike. Together this identifies potential therapeutic targets for SARS-CoV-2 and reveals SARS-lineage specific and pan-coronavirus host factors that regulate susceptibility to highly pathogenic coronaviruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-15T21:29:14.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9x47wzy8x7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9x47wzy8x7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Craig","last_name":"Wilen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR","Middle East Respiratory Syndrome-Related Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"4g722x4jfg","title":"Western blot","description":"Western blot of CRISPR-Cas9-mediated nontargeting (NT) versus BACH2 knockout 3 days after CRISPR-Cas9-gRNA nucleofection of human gut cells.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-07-02T21:20:55.966Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/4g722x4jfg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/4g722x4jfg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yulong","last_name":"Wei","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Gut","Western Blot","T Cell","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":143299,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":183,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":143299,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":142468,"restricted":72,"embargoed":759},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2346},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1345},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":716},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":476},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":444},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":429},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":415},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":398},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":339},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":281}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":140635},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1910},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":185},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":142},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":79},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":58},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":42},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":28}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":872},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":782},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":540},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":410},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","na